MOUNT(2)                       P32W Unix                        MOUNT(2)

NAME
       mount, umount - mount or unmount a filesystem

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/mount.h>

       int mount(const char *special, const char *dir, int flags);
       int umount(const char *special);

DESCRIPTION
       mount attaches the filesystem on the block device special
       (such as /dev/ram) to the directory dir: from then on dir
       stands for the root of that filesystem, and .. from that
       root leads to dir's parent. Whatever dir held is hidden
       until the filesystem is unmounted. flags is ignored. Only
       the super-user may mount.

       The device must hold a P32W filesystem (mkfs(8)). If it was
       not unmounted cleanly, the kernel prints a warning on the
       console advising fsck(8); it is mounted all the same. While
       mounted, a filesystem is marked not clean.

       umount detaches the filesystem on special. Its buffered
       blocks are written out and it is marked clean. It must not
       be in use: no open file, current directory or other
       reference may be on it.

       Besides the root, at most three filesystems can be mounted
       at once. The kernel keeps no table for users to read:
       mount(8) records mounts in /etc/mtab.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EPERM   The caller is not the super-user.
       ENOTBLK special is not a block device.
       ENXIO   special names a block device that does not exist.
       EBUSY   (mount) the device is already mounted or is the
               root device; dir already has a filesystem mounted
               on it, or is a root directory. (umount) a file on
               the filesystem is in use.
       ENOTDIR dir is not a directory, or a component of a path
               prefix is not a directory.
       EINVAL  (mount) the device holds no P32W filesystem (bad
               magic number). (umount) special is not mounted, or
               is the root.
       ENOMEM  (mount) the mount table is full.
       EIO     (mount) the superblock could not be read.
       ENOENT  special or dir does not exist.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in a
               path.
       EFAULT  A path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               A path is 256 characters or more, or a component
               is longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       mount(8), umount(8), mkfs(8), fsck(8), mtab(5), sync(2)

BUGS
       There is no read-only mount (flags is unused; EROFS never
       happens). umount takes the device, not the directory. The
       root filesystem cannot be unmounted.
